{
  "format": "wspradar.config",
  "schema_version": 1,
  "profile": {
    "id": "milazzo_tx_buddy",
    "title": {
      "en": "Dr. Milazzo Revisited: Extending an Early Multi-Part WSPR Antenna Study with Same-Cycle Evidence"
    },
    "description": {
      "en": "[Technical article: Milazzo, C. F. / KP4MD (2011). Using the Weak Signal Propagation Reporter Network to Compare Antenna Performance](https://www.qsl.net/kp4md/wspr.htm)\n\nRevisits the **40 m KP4MD full-wave loop versus WB6RQN GAP Titan DX transmit comparison** for **19 December 2010 12:00–20 December 2010 20:00 UTC**, extending the analysis to receivers within **5,000 km**.\n\nIn **Segment Insight → Joint-Spot ΔSNR**, **45 Joint observations across 30 receivers and nine shared cycles** give mean **−3.96 dB** and median **−4 dB**, favoring WB6RQN after reported-power normalization.\n\nSelect **VE6PDQ (DO34ir)** in **Station Insights**: **1 Joint Spot, 56 Only KP4MD and 0 Only WB6RQN**. Eighteen additional WB6RQN reports fail the **Target-Active Gate** because no qualifying receiver reported KP4MD in those cycles. The gate retains a cycle only when KP4MD was reported by at least one receiver; it does not require both transmitters to be heard at every receiver.\n\nThe Joint pair at **20 December 00:22 UTC** illustrates normalization to 1 W: KP4MD’s **−13 dB at 5 W** becomes **−20 dB**, while WB6RQN’s **−15 dB at 2 W** becomes **−18 dB**. Thus **ΔSNR = −2 dB**.\n\nThis agrees with the article’s description of WB6RQN being **2–6 dB stronger in Figure 7**. Archived station identities indicate that its printed direction is reversed: Figure 7 mixes up TX and RX, describing transmissions **from VE6PDQ** that were actually received **at VE6PDQ**. \n\n KP4MD operated continuously on the frequency while WB6RQN frequency-hopped, so non-joint counts reflect operating schedules as well as reception and cannot isolate antenna performance. Only **Joint Spots** enter the ΔSNR comparison: both transmitters must be decoded at the same receiving station and locator in the same WSPR cycle. This narrower synchronized subset, with reported-power normalization, compares relative SNR on the sampled paths; it does not establish direction-independent antenna gain.\n\n[Review Figure 7 with reconstructed reports and Target-Active Gate markers (PDF)](app/static/reference_figures/milazzo_publication_overlays_v1/figure7_tx_overlay.pdf). [Review the reciprocal RX comparison in Figure 6 (PDF)](app/static/reference_figures/milazzo_publication_overlays_v1/figure6_rx_overlay.pdf)."
    }
  },
